WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.900
Hi! Welcome to this video tutorial from the Audiovisual Media Production Center

2
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.900
of the Universidad Autónoma de Occidente. My name is Juan Daniel Sánchez and I'll be

3
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:34.500
telling you about the basic use of our equipment. Today we start with the Sony Z90 camera.

4
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.900
To begin, let's talk a little about its structure. The Z90 is a camcorder, a video camera

5
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.900
that is very small and light. Its lens is fixed and it has an optical zoom.

6
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.900
On top of its body it has a removable handle with two XLR inputs

7
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.900
that support professional stereo audio recording.

8
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.900
On the left side it has an LCD screen that works as a monitor.

9
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.900
When you open it, you'll find the control buttons and the memory card slots.

10
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.900
On the back of the camera you'll find the space to mount the battery,

11
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.900
the viewfinder and a control, the multi-selector, that lets us

12
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:39.500
move through the whole camera menu.

13
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.500
The first thing we need to know to operate the camera is how to mount

14
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.900
the battery on the back. We do it by sliding the lock at the bottom while

15
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.900
inserting the battery with its connectors facing up. We make sure

16
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.500
it's in place by releasing the lock. To insert the memory card correctly

17
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.900
we must open the LCD screen and open this small door, which lets us see

18
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.900
the slots where the memory card goes. We check the shape marked by the camera's guide

19
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.900
and insert it until it makes a small click and the card

20
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.900
is all the way in.

21
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.900
To turn the camera on we simply open the LCD screen, but if

22
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.900
the screen is already open and the camera is off, we must press this

23
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.900
power button on the left side. In this same area you'll find all the

24
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.900
buttons to control the camera's functions. To navigate the settings we press the

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.500
MENU button and move with the multi-selector on the back.

26
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:24.500
One of the most important things to keep in mind when recording with our

27
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.900
Sony Z90 is choosing the right recording format. To find the recording formats, the first thing

28
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.900
we do is press the MENU button. With the multi-selector we select the second

29
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.900
menu from the top, called Recording Settings. We press the selector to enter and again

30
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.900
to open the format options. The most recommended recording format, which works with

31
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:58.500
any type of memory card, is AVCHD. To change the exposure settings

32
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.900
of the Z90 we use these buttons at the bottom which, as their names

33
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:13.900
say, let us adjust the camera's iris, ISO and shutter. To change each of them

34
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.900
press it, adjust it with the selector on the back, and press the selector when it's set.

35
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.900
Be careful, because sometimes we press the button, adjust the exposure,

36
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:35.500
but then press the button again, and that switches the setting back to automatic.

37
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.900
A very useful tool that will help us a lot when recording in

38
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.900
bright light or outdoors is the ND filter. These filters are located on the

39
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.900
back of the camera. Each time we move it up, a filter is added to the image,

40
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:03.500
which reduces the light entering the camera and lets us adjust the exposure better.

41
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:12.900
To do a correct white balance we press the WHITE BALANCE button,

42
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.900
choose option A or B to save our balance, and then go to the menu, to the

43
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:27.900
Camera Settings option. We enter WB SET, zoom in on something white that

44
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:35.900
has the same lighting as the place where we'll record, and execute by pressing the multi-selector.

45
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.900
Finally, we press MENU to exit that setting.

46
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:49.500
The Z90 has a hybrid autofocus with 273 reference points,

47
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.900
which means the focus is very fast, very intuitive, and it also recognizes

48
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.900
faces very easily. To turn it on, just press the button

49
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.900
on the left side of the lens and you'll immediately see on the screen the symbol

50
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.900
that shows autofocus is active. If you want to go back to manual focus

51
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:22.900
press the button again and adjust with the lens ring. If the lens ring

52
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:29.900
moves the zoom instead of the focus even in manual, change the switch

53
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:38.500
next to it to the FOCUS position. To set up sound recording on our Z90 with an

54
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:47.900
XLR microphone, first make sure these switches are

55
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.900
set to MIC, and then go to the main menu. Go to the Audio Settings menu,

56
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:01.900
enter Microphone Select and choose MI SHOE MIC to get the audio

57
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:10.500
from the XLR microphone. Watch out: often, after recording the interviews, when we

58
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:16.900
move on to the supporting shots we turn off the microphone or leave it on the subject,

59
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.900
but those supporting shots will either have no audio or

60
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.900
have the interviewee's voice in the background. So my recommendation is that when recording

61
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.900
supporting shots, go back to the audio settings and select the camera's internal microphone

62
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:38.900
so the shots keep ambient sound. To learn more about using these

63
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.900
microphones and setting them up on the Z90, watch the complete video tutorial we made

64
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:52.900
about sound.

65
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:59.900
My first tip is about a function we haven't talked about: this

66
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.900
small switch on the back of the camera labeled AUTO/MANUAL.

67
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.900
This switch sets all of the camera's functions to automatic,

68
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:15.900
which means we can't change any of the settings. My second tip

69
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.900
is the stabilizer. This camera has a very good stabilizer for when we need

70
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.900
to shoot handheld or follow a walk, and we can turn it on from the MENU.

71
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:35.900
Go to Camera Settings and select SteadyShot. When it's on, in

72
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.900
either of its two modes the image is slightly cropped, but it helps our handheld

73
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:50.500
shots. Tip number three: although the Z90's autofocus is very good and intuitive at recognizing

74
00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.900
faces, in most cases we should keep focus in manual. A tool that

75
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:02.900
helps as a focus assistant is peaking, which you can turn on in the

76
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.900
Display Settings menu. Enter Peaking and turn it on. You'll then see

77
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:15.900
colored dots on the image that show where the camera is in focus. Note

78
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:24.500
that these dots are not recorded in the shot; they are just a focus aid.

79
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:31.900
My last tip, although it may seem obvious, is to remember that this camera has

80
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.900
two buttons to start recording. The first one is this one on the

81
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:44.900
back, and the second one is on top, on the handle. Thank you for watching

82
00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.900
this video tutorial from the UAO Audiovisual Media Production Center. We recommend watching

83
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.900
our other videos to learn more about the equipment available to you.

84
00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.900
If you have any questions, you can write to us on our social media.

85
00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:07.500
See you in the next video tutorial.
